From 7d7ac93aae1897fde180f75c480defb6034573a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 11:53:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: rockchip: rework the Crypto V2 driver and add the
 RV1126

Bring the RK3588 driver up to the current crypto API and add the
RV1126. The RK3568, RK3588 and RV1126 share the register interface;
only the RV1126 has been tested, with the kernel's full self-tests
including the randomised comparison against the generic implementations.

The driver directory is entered for CONFIG_CRYPTO_DEV_ROCKCHIP2 as well,
so the driver is built without the RK3288 one.

Ciphers run one descriptor per contiguous segment of the request, with the
chaining value kept in software between segments: the hardware does not
carry it from one descriptor of a list to the next. XTS requests with
more than one segment, AES-192, partial blocks, misaligned or too long
requests use the software implementation. Hashes are handed to the
hardware as one descriptor list per digest; the other hash operations
use the software implementation. The completion is signalled by the
interrupt that the last descriptor raises, errors reset the cipher core,
and the key registers are cleared after use. AES in ECB, CBC, CTR and
XTS modes and the MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256 and SHA-512 digests are offered.

On the RV1126 a 64 KiB CBC request runs at about 214 MB/s, ECB and CTR
at about 420 MB/s and SHA-256 at about 175 MB/s, against 19, 20 and
35 MB/s for the generic implementations.

The debug statistics, the register dumps and the device list are
dropped, the hardware byte swap is enabled as the data and key
registers expect big endian words, an optional reset is pulsed at
probe, and the clocks are gated through runtime PM.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
